-- ============================================================================
-- 06a - Shared transaction view
-- ============================================================================
-- One transaction with everything a detail list needs already joined, and with
-- the ids of the things it hangs off exposed as strings.
--
-- Two screens read it: the cashflow inspector (10a) and the dashboard drilldown
-- (21). It used to live with the dashboard, but it is not a dashboard thing.
--
-- Why ids as strings. A view entity cannot carry an association (CE6771,
-- FINDINGS 41), and Mendix XPath has no id operator reachable from a microflow
-- expression, so `[id = $Text]` is not available either (MDL048). What *is*
-- available is `cast(id as string)`: the value is stable, it is identical
-- across independently defined views, and constraining a view on that column
-- returns exactly the rows an association join returns — verified against the
-- association path before this was built on (FINDINGS 74).
--
-- That is what makes the id a usable key: a caller that holds a CategoryId
-- string can fetch these rows without ever holding a Category object. It
-- replaces matching on display names, which was only ever correct while no two
-- categories shared a name.
--
-- Casted ids must be declared string(200): a derived column normalises to that
-- length regardless of the source, and declaring anything else is CE6770 "View
-- Entity is out of sync with the OQL Query" (FINDINGS 36, 74).
-- ============================================================================

/**
 * A transaction with its category, group and account resolved.
 *
 * Mirrors are excluded here rather than at every call site — an internal
 * transfer books on both accounts, and no detail list wants both halves.
 *
 * Yr and MonthIndex are plain integers off TxDate rather than a date range, so
 * a page can constrain on them in XPath — which is what lets the cashflow
 * inspector be a database datasource instead of a microflow, and that is what
 * keeps a cell click from rebuilding the whole matrix (docs/observability.md).
 *
 * Sign and AbsAmount exist so a text template can render '-€ 61.38' rather than
 * '€ -61.38'. A parameter's format block gives precision and group digits but no
 * currency symbol and no control over where the minus goes, so the split is done
 * in the query: the template is '{1}€ {2}'.
 *
 * Amount and SignedAmount are both carried: callers total on Amount (which is
 * always positive, matching how the matrix and KPIs add up) and display
 * SignedAmount (which carries the direction).
 *
 * GroupTypeName ('Income' or 'Expense') is cast from the enum, the same way
 * VMatrixActual derives it. It lets a caller separate the two without matching
 * on a group's display name, which is only correct until someone renames one.
 *
 * **Uncategorised transactions are in here too, as a second UNION ALL branch
 * carrying CategoryName 'Needs review'.** The dashboard counts twelve of them
 * and had no way to list them: every consumer of this view reached transactions
 * through an inner join on Category, so a transaction without one was invisible
 * everywhere except the count that complained about it. Putting them in the
 * view rather than in a second entity means the dashboard's inspector, its
 * click-through and its detail chart all work on them with no special case —
 * which is the same argument the dashboard already makes for giving them a row
 * in the table rather than a badge in a corner.
 *
 * They carry GroupTypeName 'Review' rather than 'Expense', deliberately: every
 * existing consumer either constrains on CategoryId (the cashflow inspector and
 * its drill) or filters on GroupTypeName = 'Expense' (the Insights scatter, the
 * day-click list), so none of them sees a new row. That was checked against
 * each caller before the branch was added, not after.
 *
 * A left join with `where c.Name = null` is how OQL asks for "no category" —
 * verified to return exactly the twelve.
 *
 * Every string column is cast and declared string(200), because a literal or
 * derived column normalises to 200 regardless of source (finding 36) and the
 * two branches must agree. Before the branch existed these were pass-throughs
 * declaring their source's exact length — CategoryGroup.Name string(50),
 * Category.Name and Account.Name string(100) — and mixing those with a literal
 * is CE6770 "View Entity is out of sync with the OQL Query", which names the
 * entity and not the column (finding 97).
 *
 * Description rides along for the transaction popup. It is the one field a
 * detail view has that a list row does not.
 */
create or modify view entity Ledger.VTransactionLine (
  GroupId: string(200),
  GroupName: string(200),
  GroupTypeName: string(200),
  CategoryId: string(200),
  CategoryName: string(200),
  Merchant: string(200),
  AccountName: string(200),
  Description: string(500),
  TxDate: datetime,
  Yr: integer,
  MonthIndex: integer,
  Amount: decimal,
  SignedAmount: decimal,
  Sign: string(200),
  AbsAmount: decimal
) as (
  select
    cast(g.id as string) as GroupId,
    cast(g.Name as string) as GroupName,
    cast(g.GroupType as string) as GroupTypeName,
    cast(c.id as string) as CategoryId,
    cast(c.Name as string) as CategoryName,
    cast(t.Merchant as string) as Merchant,
    cast(a.Name as string) as AccountName,
    t.Description as Description,
    t.TxDate as TxDate,
    datepart(YEAR, t.TxDate) as Yr,
    datepart(MONTH, t.TxDate) as MonthIndex,
    t.Amount as Amount,
    t.SignedAmount as SignedAmount,
    (case when t.SignedAmount < 0 then '-' else '' end) as Sign,
    (case when t.SignedAmount < 0 then 0 - t.SignedAmount else t.SignedAmount end) as AbsAmount
  from Ledger.Transaction as t
  inner join t/Ledger.Transaction_Category/Ledger.Category as c
  inner join c/Ledger.Category_CategoryGroup/Ledger.CategoryGroup as g
  inner join t/Ledger.Transaction_Account/Ledger.Account as a
  where t.IsMirror = false
  union all
  select
    cast('' as string) as GroupId,
    cast('Review' as string) as GroupName,
    cast('Review' as string) as GroupTypeName,
    cast('' as string) as CategoryId,
    cast('Needs review' as string) as CategoryName,
    cast(t.Merchant as string) as Merchant,
    cast(a.Name as string) as AccountName,
    t.Description as Description,
    t.TxDate as TxDate,
    datepart(YEAR, t.TxDate) as Yr,
    datepart(MONTH, t.TxDate) as MonthIndex,
    t.Amount as Amount,
    t.SignedAmount as SignedAmount,
    (case when t.SignedAmount < 0 then '-' else '' end) as Sign,
    (case when t.SignedAmount < 0 then 0 - t.SignedAmount else t.SignedAmount end) as AbsAmount
  from Ledger.Transaction as t
  inner join t/Ledger.Transaction_Account/Ledger.Account as a
  left join t/Ledger.Transaction_Category/Ledger.Category as c
  where t.IsMirror = false and c.Name = null
);
/
